WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.350
This is St Peter's Seminary, about an hour west
of Glasgow in Scotland.

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:06.250
Or rather, it was.

00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:09.320
It was built in the 1960s to be a
training school for priests,

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:12.200
and I think it's a beautiful
ruin of a building.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:14.920
But then, I like Modernist
architecture like this.

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:18.420
In 1992, this was put on the list
of historic Scottish buildings

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:22.550
in category A, which means that it's of
national or international importance.

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:27.730
And architecturally, it is one of the most
important modern buildings in Scotland.

00:00:27.730 --> 00:00:31.110
But, by the time it was listed,
it had already been closed for years.

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:34.870
The Catholic church had decided that
priests should train in towns and cities,

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:36.810
not in remote places like this.

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:38.380
And besides, times were changing.

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:40.940
There weren't as many people who
wanted to be priests.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:43.820
No one was interested in buying the place,
and the church couldn't afford to keep

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:47.900
an empty and extremely high-maintenance
building running for no purpose.

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:49.800
One of the troubles with a unique
structure like this

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:52.640
is that it's expensive and difficult
to keep repaired.

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:57.130
So, a quarter of a century later,
what's left looks... rough.

00:00:57.130 --> 00:01:00.060
- The Archdiocese of Glasgow
has been responsible for it

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:01.900
since the moment it opened.

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:04.060
It was building which was very
difficult to reuse,

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:06.070
because it had been custom built.

00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:10.740
No developer wanted a building
that had a huge, concrete chapel in it.

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:16.800
We have a responsibility to try and
preserve the ruin, as best we can.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:22.320
It is now a ruin. It's covered in graffiti,
it's inaccessible, it's dangerous.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:27.500
We have, by statute, to try and
maintain some kind of security

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:30.080
in that area, to insure it and so on.

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:32.980
But after 40 years, we are
at our wits' end.

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:38.000
In 40 years we have worked with
every imaginable idea.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:41.760
Developers have thought of
turning it into a community centre,

00:01:41.762 --> 00:01:45.450
or a hotel complex, and
everything in between.

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:48.063
There were inherent weaknesses
in the building.

00:01:48.063 --> 00:01:53.410
Those who lived there have nightmarish
stories of water ingress, for example,

00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:58.166
of the fact that the wind would
blow so strongly through the windows,

00:01:58.166 --> 00:02:02.060
that curtains would be lifted to be
almost horizontal.

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:05.960
So the building itself was not
an easy building to live in or work in,

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:09.590
which made it even more difficult
to find an alternative use for it.

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:12.580
- One arts organisation spent millions
on making it safer,

00:02:12.580 --> 00:02:16.160
removing asbestos and old fittings,
using it as a stage for light shows,

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:18.820
hoping to turn it into an arts venue.

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:20.170
But then they ran out of money.

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:23.280
And besides, by design, this building's
in the middle of nowhere.

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:25.480
There's a village a mile down the road,
but that's it.

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:27.720
There are better places for arts venues.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:30.260
And there's no way in law to just
abandon a building like this.

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:32.500
You can 'tjust decide that it doesn't
spark joy anymore

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:34.680
and donate it to a charity shop.

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:36.610
And if definitely can't be knocked down,

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:39.490
because it's a really significant,
listed building.

00:02:39.490 --> 00:02:42.980
- So it's not just a listed building,
it's a grade A listed building.

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:47.270
It's the highest form of listing possible,
and we are required to stick to

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:51.410
all sorts of rules about not using
it for any alternative purpose,

00:02:51.410 --> 00:02:54.790
not amending it, trying to keep
the area around safe.

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:59.970
Legally, we would give that building away,
with the estate, but whoever takes it on

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:06.180
takes on responsibility for insurance,
for security, for upkeep and so on.

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:09.170
So it's not so easy.
You literally can't give it away.

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:12.480
There have been expressions of interest,
but when it comes down to it,

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:15.150
it's a question of money, of finance.

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:19.440
Because the building itself swallows
vast amounts of money,

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:22.486
and even to maintain it
in its current ruined state,

00:03:22.486 --> 00:03:26.900
costs the Archdiocese
something like £60,000 a year.

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:31.540
People have looked at the area around,
because it's on a beautiful estate.

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:33.770
They have looked at perhaps
building houses there

00:03:33.770 --> 00:03:35.370
and using the profit from that.

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:40.000
But that has been denied because there are
greenbelt issues which prevent that, too.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:43.490
And so there's something tragic about it,
there's something haunting about it.

00:03:43.490 --> 00:03:47.440
It's unrealistic to expect some
sort of deus ex machina.

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:52.940
We remain open to working with anyone who
wants to come forward with plans, with ideas,

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:57.210
but at the same time, realistically,
this is bigger than us.

00:03:57.210 --> 00:04:00.030
This is something for the state,
something for the nation.

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:03.300
And most people would probably say,

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:05.220
"what a mess".

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:08.970
Those that understand buildings,
and that have an understanding of

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:15.800
brutalist architecture, would see it
as an extraordinary treasure.

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:17.740
That's the reality.

00:04:17.740 --> 00:04:19.000
It is both.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:21.100
- If this was centuries old,
rather than decades,

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:22.460
it'd be a national treasure.

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:24.560
The Scottish government would probably
pay to restore it.

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:25.630
You could charge an admission fee.

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:28.080
Parents would bring their kids
to picnic in the grounds.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:31.300
And the idea of spraying graffiti
on it would be abhorrent.

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:33.720
But, apparently, it isn't.

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:38.730
Despite the fact that this is a
one-of-a-kind historic religious building.

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:42.450
This place has enormous historical
and cultural value

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:44.960
but a negative financial value.

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:48.333
And that, it turns out, is
a very difficult place to be in.

